data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Considering that only 4 transactions can be open concurrently and a single transaction can have a vast number of read/range records, the read and range query behavior under these constraints requires careful examination. The high transaction volume, coupled with the limited number of open transactions, suggests a potential bottleneck or performance degradation with many concurrent reads and range queries. The interaction between snapshot isolation, range query efficiency, and the transaction limits is now the focal point of analysis.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The initial approach of copying the entire state on `BEGIN` is computationally infeasible given the high frequency of transactions; the operation count would be excessive.  A persistent balanced binary search tree, where each node stores subtree size, appears promising for maintaining snapshots and handling range counts efficiently. `BEGIN` operations can be optimized to $O(1)$ by simply saving the current root pointer, and `COMMIT` operations are bounded by the number of writes.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Considering garbage collection of unreferenced nodes. If no other transactions reference a particular tree version, and a transaction commits, older versions become immediately eligible for collection, which would happen automatically.  Path-copying AVL trees combined with Java GC would handle transaction snapshots efficiently. With 500,000 commits, 20 node allocations per write are manageable in a 512MB heap.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

