data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Analyzing memory allocation under the observed transaction load, a short-lived object scenario necessitates about 5ms of garbage collection time. Implementing epoch tagging for AVL nodes allows in-place mutation of unshared nodes, significantly reducing memory allocation to near zero even with numerous concurrent transactions. This optimized persistent AVL tree, combined with an epoch-based copy-on-write strategy, offers a performant solution.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"txn 2 holds epoch 5 root. Considering the standard path-copying AVL implementation without mutation, code complexity is low and the approach is safe for concurrent access. Calculation indicates a manageable memory footprint, including only 360MB young-gen allocations for 500,000 writes, collected quickly.\n\nREAD operations involve a rapid transaction write-list check and then a lookup in the snapshot root, bounded by comparisons. RANGE operations will use subtree size information for efficient counting within specified bounds.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"READ and RANGE operations are optimized for $O(\\log N)$ performance. RANGE queries efficiently count within specified bounds using subtree size information. The write list adjustment handles conflicts correctly: keys are deduplicated, and counts are updated based on `PUT` or `DELETE` operations. Assuming each comparison is $O(1)$, which is the case, the tree steps would take ~50-100 ms in Java, well within acceptable bounds.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Analyzing conflict definition R22. Considering scenarios where transaction `T` executes numerous reads or ranges, and other transactions `U` commit. The critical factor for conflict appears to be overlap between `T`'s read set/ranges and `U`'s written keys, regardless of the timing within the `T`'s duration. The exact point of read within the transaction does not directly determine conflicts.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

