data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The read set of a transaction is defined by its `READ` records; rollbacks don't affect this. Similarly, read ranges are defined by `RANGE` records; rollbacks also don't affect these. A conflict occurs when a transaction's read set/ranges overlap with the written keys of a concurrently committed transaction whose commit falls between the current transaction's begin and commit. Determining conflicts involves checking if any committed transaction's writes intersect with the read set or ranges.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Conflict determination now clarified. A monotonically increasing `commitSeq` is assigned to each committed transaction `U`. During `T.BEGIN`, `T.beginCommitSeq` stores the current `commitSeq`.  Transactions `U` with `commitSeq` greater than `T.beginCommitSeq` and less than or equal to the current `commitSeq` at `T.COMMIT` are the conflicting transactions. A global `lastWriteCommitSeq` map, keyed by each written key `K`, tracks the maximum `commitSeq` for any transaction that wrote `K`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"BST augmented with `maxSubtreeSeq` will be evaluated for conflict detection during range queries. The approach hinges on establishing an exact equivalence between the existence of a conflicting key within a range and the maximal `lastSeq` of any key within the range. This involves verifying that if the maximum `lastSeq` for keys within a given range exceeds the transaction's `beginCommitSeq`, a conflict exists. Specifically, the test involves checking if the maximum of `lastSeq(K)` for keys `K` within `[lo, hi]` is greater than `T.beginCommitSeq`, which means there's a conflict.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checking the computational complexity of conflict detection. If a transaction has many read or range operations, conflict resolution performance becomes critical. The strategy involves querying the `lastWriteCommitSeq` BST for each read or range operation within the transaction to determine if the read or range set conflicts with other committed transactions. The potential for a large number of these queries now requires careful scrutiny.\n\nFurther examining the approach to handle the impact of numerous read and range operations. If the transaction has 500,000 reads and ranges, the complexity becomes $O(N \\log W)$ or even $O(N)$ with a `HashMap`. With an AVL implementation, we could just check each range for maxSeq to determine conflicts. Coalescing ranges or using an array of ranges for conflict determination might offer optimization.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

