data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial analysis indicates a need to re-evaluate the order of R15 and R16 validation to align with specification R13 through R16a. `PUT` and `READ (f=1)` records necessitate validation of `BAD_KEY` (R15) before `BAD_VALUE` (R16). The existing validation order precisely matches the sequential order specified, ensuring correct handling of these constraints. Considering Phase 2, the next task is applying transaction checks.\n\nNow analyzing R17, which relates to duplicate transaction IDs in `BEGIN` records.  It checks for an earlier BEGIN record.  Considered: if a previous BEGIN record failed validation (R13, R14, R17, or R18), the process throws an exception and halts, so the duplicate check is unnecessary.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R17's duplicate transaction ID check simplified: earlier failed `BEGIN` validation halts processing; no redundant check needed. Considering storing seen `txn` IDs. A `HashSet<Integer>` would be costly; an open-addressed `IntHashSet` offers efficient, allocation-free storage of non-zero `int` transaction IDs, requiring minimal memory and constant-time lookup for R17's validation.\n\nR17's duplicate check now uses an `IntHashSet` to efficiently track `BEGIN` transaction IDs. This open-addressed hash set avoids object allocation overhead, ensuring constant-time lookups and supporting millions of transactions with a small memory footprint, fulfilling the performance requirements. Employing a good hash mix algorithm prevents adversarial input collisions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Open transaction limit validation implemented using a fixed-size array (`openTxns[4]`). This approach replaces the hash set, eliminating object allocation overhead and enabling constant-time checks. The `Txn` objects are now efficiently reused from a pool, ensuring no garbage collection issues.\n\nR19 (TXN_NOT_OPEN) now validated by a direct array lookup of open transactions (up to 4 entries), eliminating the prior hash set and optimizing lookups for maximum performance. R19a validates the write limit (8 writes maximum) within an open transaction to handle R19a (TXN_TOO_LARGE).\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

