data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`LinkedHashMap` initialized with `stateRoot.size` (from AVL) guarantees deterministic iteration and minimal memory usage; a `Txn` object pool of size four is now pre-allocated with efficient reset/clear methods to eliminate object allocations, optimizing garbage collection by using `HashSet.clear()` and checking if `readKeys.size() > 256`, and in that case replacing readKeys, to free underlying memory. The pool size is set to the constant `MAX_OPEN_TXNS = 4`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`readRanges = new TreeMap<>()` is used if `readRanges.size() > 256` to ensure low memory footprint and avoid reallocation for numerous small transactions. The `Txn` reset optimization is re-evaluated: `readKeys` allocation is lazy, using a `HashSet` with `.clear()` for $O(1)$ if empty. `HashMap.clear()` on OpenJDK scans the *entire* `tab.length` if `size > 0`, which is verified and confirmed. If `readKeysSizePeak > 256`, replacing `readKeys = null` frees the table, preventing a large scan on subsequent small transactions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-dde1abc0-d722-43c0-bc55-6df37dad804e","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795555059,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The potential for hash table bloat with frequent `clear()` calls identified, particularly with large initial sizes, necessitates careful handling of the `readKeys` hash set within transactions. Replacing the hash set with null on `clear()` resolves the potential quadratic slowdown, ensuring the performance is maintained, and memory consumption is optimized. The lazy initialization of `readKeys` and `readRanges` on first use further simplifies memory management, effectively mitigating the concern. With that and the pool size, the code will be very performant and correct.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

